---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
---
<Layout title="Privacy — TabMonger" description="Privacy information for the TabMonger website, dashboard, and browser companions.">
  <main class="legal shell">
    <a href="/" class="brand-link"><Logo /></a>
    <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Privacy</p>
    <h1>The short version: useful totals, no advertising profile.</h1>
    <p class="legal-lede">The TabMonger website keeps small first-party aggregate counters so the project owner can understand traffic, downloads, and community interest. It uses no advertising pixels, analytics company, user account, tracking cookie, or third-party JavaScript. The downloaded dashboard and browser companions remain telemetry-free.</p>
    <section>
      <h2>Aggregate website metrics</h2>
      <p>For each allowlisted website event, the server stores only the UTC date, a general action such as a page view or download click, and a coarse source category such as direct, search, GitHub, or Reddit. Raw referral URLs never leave your browser. The analytics store does not contain IP addresses, one-way source hashes, cookies, browser identifiers, user agents, full URLs, query strings, or fingerprints.</p>
      <p>A salted one-way source hash may exist briefly in server memory to rate-limit abuse. It is not written with an analytics event. Aggregate records expire after 180 days and are used only to improve TabMonger, not to build or sell advertising profiles.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Ordinary server logs</h2>
      <p>The web host may temporarily record standard technical request information such as IP address, requested path, browser user agent, and timestamp for reliability and abuse prevention.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>One-time support</h2>
      <p>If you choose to support TabMonger, you leave this site for a Stripe-hosted checkout. Stripe processes the payment under its own privacy policy. TabMonger’s website does not receive or store your card or bank details.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Community feedback and feature poll</h2>
      <p>The optional community form asks for a short title and message, but not your name or email address. General feedback stays private. A feature request remains private and pending until the project owner reviews it; if approved, only its title and vote total appear in the public poll. Submitted details, moderation status, and abuse-prevention data are never returned by the public poll API.</p>
      <p>The server briefly uses a salted one-way source hash—not the raw network address—in memory to rate-limit spam; it is not saved with your message. It stores only a one-way hash of the random voting identifier kept in your browser. Your browser also saves the selected poll-item ID so it can mark your current choice. These values are used only to record, remember, and change that choice; neither identifier is included in the aggregate website metrics.</p>
      <p>Poll totals may include clearly disclosed project-owner starter votes. Starter votes are stored separately from anonymous browser votes and are never represented as individual visitors.</p>
      <p>Rejected submissions, reviewed general feedback, and the private source material behind approved requests are automatically removed after 30 days. Unreviewed submissions expire after 180 days. Approved feature titles and anonymous votes remain public while that poll option is active; closing an option removes its votes immediately and keeps its title privately for at most 30 more days. Protected deployment backups are kept for no longer than 30 days.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>The dashboard app</h2>
      <p>Your self-hosted TabMonger installation keeps its links, settings, icons, background, and backups on your own server. It includes no telemetry.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Browser new-tab companions</h2>
      <p>The Chrome, Edge, and Firefox companions store only your TabMonger base address, two local choices—whether new-tab redirection is enabled and whether to check availability first—and a host-permission pattern derived from that address so old access can be removed after an address change. This information stays in the browser’s extension storage and is not sent to TabMonger’s website or any analytics service.</p>
      <p>If you enable the friendly offline check, the browser asks for optional access to the saved address’s HTTP or HTTPS hostname. The companion uses that permission only to request TabMonger’s read-only <code>/api/health</code> route without credentials. It does not read browsing history or dashboard content, and it contains no telemetry, advertising, payment code, or remote scripts.</p>
    </section>
    <a class="button" href="/">← Return home</a>
  </main>
</Layout>
